# rustdesk-server docker-compose.yml — self-hosted RustDesk rendezvous (hbbs) + relay (hbbr), HD-412
#   The family-support introduction point. Placement is DECIDED and logged in
#   docs/services-rejected.md (2026-09-20): the server lives on the **VPS**, never on oldsrv
#   (a rescue tool behind the thing it rescues is not a rescue, and making it reachable at home
#   would need a public listener on the crown-jewel host). Full owning section:
#   docs/services-admin.md §RustDesk.
#
#   IMAGE = rustdesk/rustdesk-server-s6 — the SUPERVISOR image (busybox:stable + s6-overlay
#   3.2.0.0, verified from the upstream Dockerfile at the pinned tag 2026-09-21). It runs hbbs and
#   hbbr as two supervised services in ONE container sharing one working dir (/data), which is what
#   makes the key model work: both binaries `cd /data` and load the SAME id_ed25519 keypair, and
#   s6 orders hbbs after `key-secret` + hbbr. The non-s6 image (rustdesk/rustdesk-server) is two
#   containers with no supervisor and none of the env conveniences below.
#     * WorkingDir /data (declared VOLUME), Entrypoint /init, ExposedPorts 21115-21119.
#     * HEALTHCHECK is baked into the image (`s6-svstat` on the hbbs + hbbr service dirs), so it is
#       INHERITED, not overridden: unlike the whisper leg (HD-391, where the image's hardcoded
#       HEALTHCHECK lied), this one checks supervision state, which is exactly the signal we have —
#       hbbs/hbbr expose no HTTP surface to probe.
#     * s6 republishes the CONTAINER environment into each service via `with-contenv`, so every
#       knob below must arrive as container env (a `.env` in the compose dir would NOT reach them).
#
#   network_mode: host — the CONVENTIONS §3 justification for host networking + host ports:
#     hbbs performs UDP rendezvous + TCP hole punching and must observe the REAL source
#     address/port of every client to classify NAT type and punch correctly; the NAT-type test
#     listener (21115) and the UDP 21116 heartbeat are address-sensitive by protocol. Docker's
#     userland proxy SNATs container-network traffic to the bridge gateway (172.17.0.1), which
#     destroys exactly that information — upstream's own deployment guidance is `--net=host` on
#     Linux for this reason, and says to drop `-p` when host networking works. Consequences, all
#     accepted: NO docker port publishing (so no DNAT path — every packet hits the host INPUT
#     chain, where roles/vps-hardening decides reach), NO docker networks (so no Traefik route and
#     no CrowdSec HTTP bouncer for this service), and the loopback-only admin consoles of
#     hbbs (21115) / hbbr (21117) become host-loopback-only too, which is where they belong.
#
#   REACH (enforced in roles/vps-hardening/templates/nftables.conf.j2, NOT here — the input policy
#   is drop and host-net has no DNAT): allow 21115/tcp, 21116/tcp+udp, 21117/tcp.
#   21118 (hbbs WebSocket) + 21119 (hbbr WebSocket) are DELIBERATELY NOT ALLOWED. They exist only
#   for the RustDesk **web client**, which we do not use, and upstream warns that on those two
#   ports hbbs/hbbr trust unvalidated `X-Real-IP`/`X-Forwarded-For` headers — anyone who can reach
#   them can forge the client IP recorded in logs and walk past IP-based blocking. There is also NO
#   web console here at all: the `:21114`-class HTTP console is a **Pro** feature; the OSS server is
#   configured by env/flags and administered over its loopback command console
#   (`printf 'always-use-relay Y' | nc 127.0.0.1 21115`, `printf 'h' | nc 127.0.0.1 21117`).
#   The container still BINDS 21118/21119 (upstream builds them in; the `-b/--bind` restriction
#   flag needs 1.1.17 and we pin 1.1.16), so the firewall is what closes them — see services-admin.md.
#
#   SECRETS (bulk vault pre-pass, HD-258 dict; fail-loud, no default() — HD-65):
#     rustdesk_login  Login item: `username` = the server PUBLIC key (base64),
#                              `password` = the server SECRET key (base64).
#       Generated once, OFFLINE, into the vault (procedure: deployment-manual.md §1.11). The seed
#       is one-shot by upstream design: the s6 `key-secret` step copies KEY_PUB/KEY_PRIV into
#       /data ONLY when the file does not already exist, then chmod 0600 + `rustdesk-utils
#       validatekeypair`, and HALTS the container if the pair is half-present or mismatched. So the
#       live file is authoritative while /data survives, and editing the vault item alone changes
#       nothing — which is exactly why this item is NOT_AUTO_ROTATABLE (rotating orphans every
#       enrolled client, whose saved key no longer matches). docs/deployment-secrets.md carries the
#       row + reason. ⚠ The keypair is visible in `docker inspect` output (compose env) — treat
#       inspect output on this container as secret-bearing.
#
#   BANDWIDTH is the real cost of this service, not RAM (quota arithmetic + why these numbers:
#   docs/services-admin.md §Relay bandwidth cap). The caps are the OSS hbbr knobs read at start-up
#   from the environment (verified in src/relay_server.rs at the pinned tag 1.1.16; unit = Mb/s
#   counted as 1024² bit/s). ALWAYS_USE_RELAY=N keeps sessions on hole-punched direct paths, so
#   the relay — and therefore the quota — is only spent when punching fails.
#   The five caps are SSOT'd in the registry row (group_vars/vps.yml, HD-412 region) and read as
#   svc.* here, so they stay group_vars-authored per CONVENTIONS §2.
services:
  rustdesk-server:
    image: rustdesk/rustdesk-server-s6:{{ rustdesk_server_version }}
    container_name: rustdesk-server
    restart: unless-stopped
    # Justified above (NAT/hole-punch fidelity = the upstream recommendation). No `ports:` and no
    # `networks:` — host-net makes both meaningless, and validate-docker-services.py rejects the
    # combination outright.
    network_mode: host
    cap_drop:
      - ALL
    security_opt:
      - no-new-privileges:true
    # Both binaries write only in /data (bind-mounted below); s6-overlay needs /run writable
    # (it is also /var/run via the image's symlink) and busybox wants /tmp. If a first apply
    # shows EROFS anywhere else, the honest fix is a narrower tmpfs for that path — recorded in
    # the HD-412 deploy gate, not papered over by dropping read_only.
    read_only: true
    tmpfs:
      - /tmp
      # :exec is REQUIRED, not cosmetic — s6-overlay stage0 execs /run/s6/basedir/bin/init, and
      # docker's default tmpfs opts are noexec → the container restart-loops on exit 126
      # (`/run/s6/basedir/bin/init: Permission denied`). Same finding as the HD-318 s6 batch
      # (sonarr/radarr/lidarr/qbittorrent carry `/run:exec` for exactly this reason).
      - /run:exec
    volumes:
      # THE state of this service: id_ed25519 + id_ed25519.pub (+ db_v2.sqlite3, blacklists).
      # Backup scope: docs/backup.md / Kopia (CONVENTIONS §5 step 6 — the keypair is also seeded
      # in 1Password, so a loss of /data is recoverable without re-enrolling clients).
      - /srv/docker/rustdesk-server/data:/data
    environment:
      TZ: "{{ timezone }}"
      # The s6 default is the placeholder `relay.example.com`, which hbbs would hand to every
      # client as their relay address — it MUST be overridden. The VPS public IPv4 is SSOT'd as
      # `dns_primary_ip` (group_vars/all/main.yml); no port suffix, because hbbr runs on its
      # default 21117 (this service never passes `-p`), so there is no tunable to keep in sync
      # with the nftables allow.
      RELAY: "{{ dns_primary_ip }}"
      # Key validation ON for BOTH servers: adds `-k _` to hbbs and hbbr. Without it the relay
      # runs with upstream's deliberate empty-key default, i.e. ANY client can relay through it.
      ENCRYPTED_ONLY: "1"
      # Direct (hole-punched) first; relay only as the fallback. Y here would put every session on
      # the VPS quota.
      ALWAYS_USE_RELAY: "N"
      # hbbr logs its effective caps at start-up (`TOTAL_BANDWIDTH: …Mb/s`) — that line is the
      # evidence the caps below actually took effect.
      RUST_LOG: "info"
      SINGLE_BANDWIDTH: "{{ svc.relay_single_mbps }}"
      TOTAL_BANDWIDTH: "{{ svc.relay_total_mbps }}"
      LIMIT_SPEED: "{{ svc.relay_limit_mbps }}"
      DOWNGRADE_THRESHOLD: "{{ svc.relay_downgrade_threshold }}"
      DOWNGRADE_START_CHECK: "{{ svc.relay_downgrade_start_check_s }}"
      # Compose-parsed text ⇒ escape `$` (HD-270 Population A). A RustDesk key is base64
      # (`A–Z a–z 0–9 + / =`), so the filter is a no-op today; it is here so a hand-pasted or
      # re-issued value can never be silently truncated by compose interpolation.
      KEY_PUB: >-
        {{ vault['rustdesk_login'].username | replace('$','$$') }}
      KEY_PRIV: >-
        {{ vault['rustdesk_login'].password | replace('$','$$') }}
    deploy:
      resources:
        limits:
          # Quoted string — compose's schema requires limits.memory to be a string (HD-162).
          # Upstream's own floor for this server is "a basic cloud server… you can also use a
          # Raspberry Pi"; the relay buffers are bounded by the Mb/s caps above, so this is a
          # ceiling against a runaway process, not a working-set estimate.
          memory: "512m"
